\chapter{The Rewrite Feature: Rules and Predicates}
\label{ch:rewrite}

\section{The problem: expressing a transformation without a language}

A tree is easy to build and easy to print. Turning one tree into another is
harder, because the transformation is a piece of program, and a piece of
program needs somewhere to live. Three designs are common in practice.

The first embeds the transformation in the node type: every node carries a
virtual \code{rewrite} method, and each node kind knows how to transform
itself. This makes the transformation hard to write, hard to compose, and
impossible to change without rebuilding the tree type.

The second writes the transformation as a function over the tree, with a
big \code{switch} on the node's tag. This works, but the switch has to be
rewritten for every new kind of transformation, and the two concerns ---
what a node \emph{is} and how it should be \emph{changed} --- end up in the
same function.

The third, and the one \DSLtk{} implements, separates the two. A
\emph{rule} is a self-contained pair: a predicate that decides whether the
rule applies to a node, and a transformer that produces the replacement.
Rules are ordinary values, they can be written down in isolation, they can
be tested one at a time, and they can be collected into a set and applied
repeatedly until nothing changes.

The whole mechanism is four hundred lines of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, from line
6754 to line 6941. Most of it is a single class template and a factory
function. This chapter is about the class template; the next chapter is
about the set and the fixpoint loop.

\section{A worked example}

The program below defines three rules --- double-negation elimination,
\code{x + 0} collapsing to \code{x}, and constant folding --- and applies
them to a small expression tree.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Three rewrite rules and their application.},label={lst:rewrite-demo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

using dsl::ASTNode;

bool
tag_is (ASTNode const &n, std::string_view tag)
{
  return !n.is_leaf () && n.tag () == tag;
}

bool
is_num (ASTNode const &n, std::string_view text)
{
  return n.is_leaf () && n.tag () == "num" && n.value () == text;
}

// Rule 1: not(not(x)) -> x
constexpr auto double_negation
    = dsl::rule<"elim-double-neg"> (
        [] (ASTNode const &n) {
          return tag_is (n, "not") && n.size () == 1 && tag_is (n.child (0), "not")
                 && n.child (0).size () == 1;
        },
        [] (ASTNode const &n) { return n.child (0).child (0); });

// Rule 2: add(x, 0) -> x  and  add(0, x) -> x
constexpr auto add_zero
    = dsl::rule<"add-zero"> (
        [] (ASTNode const &n) {
          if (!tag_is (n, "add") || n.size () != 2)
            return false;
          return is_num (n.child (0), "0") || is_num (n.child (1), "0");
        },
        [] (ASTNode const &n) {
          return is_num (n.child (0), "0") ? n.child (1) : n.child (0);
        });

// Rule 3: add(num a, num b) -> num (a + b)
constexpr auto fold_constants
    = dsl::rule<"fold-constants"> (
        [] (ASTNode const &n) {
          if (!tag_is (n, "add") || n.size () != 2)
            return false;
          return n.child (0).is_leaf () && n.child (0).tag () == "num"
                 && n.child (1).is_leaf () && n.child (1).tag () == "num";
        },
        [] (ASTNode const &n) {
          return dsl::leaf<"num"> (std::stoi (n.child (0).value ())
                                   + std::stoi (n.child (1).value ()));
        });

// A DSL that exposes the rule set through the Rewrite feature mixin.
struct Optimiser : dsl::DSL<Optimiser, dsl::AST, dsl::Rewrite>
{
  static inline auto rules
      = dsl::rewrite_set (double_negation, add_zero, fold_constants);
};

int
main ()
{
  // The rule name is a compile-time constant, never read by the engine.
  std::cout << "rule name: " << decltype (double_negation)::name << '\n';

  // try_apply is the only entry point: it returns nullopt when the
  // predicate rejects the node.
  ASTNode notnot = dsl::node<"not"> (dsl::node<"not"> (dsl::leaf<"x"> (1)));
  auto hit = double_negation.try_apply (notnot);
  std::cout << "try_apply: " << (hit ? hit->dump () : std::string ("no match")) << '\n';

  ASTNode plain = dsl::leaf<"x"> (1);
  std::cout << "try_apply: "
            << (double_negation.try_apply (plain) ? std::string ("match")
                                                  : std::string ("no match"))
            << '\n';

  // The whole set, applied to a tree.
  ASTNode tree = dsl::node<"add"> (dsl::node<"not"> (dsl::node<"not"> (dsl::leaf<"x"> (1))),
                                   dsl::leaf<"num"> (0));
  std::cout << "before   : " << tree.dump () << '\n';

  auto rules = dsl::rewrite_set (double_negation, add_zero, fold_constants);
  std::cout << "after    : " << rules.apply (tree).dump () << '\n';

  Optimiser o;
  std::cout << "mixin    : " << o.rewrite (tree).dump () << '\n';
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:rewrite-demo}.},label={lst:rewrite-demo-out}]
rule name: elim-double-neg
try_apply: (x 1)
try_apply: no match
before   : (add (not (not (x 1))) (num 0))
after    : (x 1)
mixin    : (x 1)
\end{lstlisting}

The result \code{"(x 1)"} is the composition of all three rules. The
inner \code{not(not(x))} is removed by the first rule; the \code{add} node
then has a \code{num 0} on the right, which the second rule removes; and
what remains is the leaf \code{(x 1)}. Which rule fires, and in what
order, is the subject of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets}.

\section{The rule type}

\api{RewriteRule} is declared at line 6768 as a three-parameter class
template. \code{Tag} is a \api{FixedString} naming the rule; \code{Pred}
and \code{Trans} are the callable types.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{5.4cm}C{1.0cm}L{6.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Semantics} \\
\midrule
\code{Pred pred}
  & 6770
  & The predicate, stored \emph{by value} in a public data member. Called
    as \code{pred (n)}, where \code{n} is a \code{const ASTNode&}. \\
\code{Trans trans}
  & 6771
  & The transformer, stored by value in a public data member. Called as
    \code{trans (n)}. \\
\code{static constexpr} \newline \code{std::string_view name}
  & 6772
  & The rule's name, taken from \code{Tag.view()}. A debugging aid. The
    engine never reads it. \\
\code{constexpr RewriteRule(Pred p, Trans t)}
  & 6774
  & Constructs the rule, moving both callables into place. \\
\code{std::optional<ASTNode>} \newline \code{try_apply(const ASTNode&) const}
  & 6784
  & The only member function. Returns the transformed node when the
    predicate accepts the argument, and \code{std::nullopt} otherwise. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The complete interface of \api{RewriteRule}. There is no
\code{apply}, no \code{matches}, and no \code{name()} accessor.}
\label{tab:rewriterule-api}
\end{table}

The absence of \code{apply} and \code{matches} is worth dwelling on,
because a rule-oriented library might reasonably provide them. The
toolkit does not, and the reason is that they would be redundant.
\code{matches(n)} would be \code{pred(n)}, and \code{pred} is a public
member: a user who wants to test a rule without transforming anything can
write \code{rule.pred (n)} directly. Likewise, \code{try_apply(n)} is the
whole of the "apply" operation. One method is enough, and the toolkit
provides one.

\begin{pseudocode}[Applying one rule to one node]
\textbf{function} try\_apply (rule, n)\\
\quad \textbf{if} rule.pred (n) \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} rule.trans (n)\\
\quad \textbf{return} empty
\end{pseudocode}

The predicate is consulted exactly once. The transformer is consulted at
most once, and only when the predicate accepted. The transformer receives
the same node the predicate saw --- not a copy, not a modified version ---
so a transformer that wants to inspect the node may do so.

\subsection{The factory}

\api{rule} is declared at line 6800. It is a thin deduction helper:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The rule factory, verbatim from the header.},label={lst:rule-impl}]
template <FixedString Tag, typename Pred, typename Trans>
constexpr auto
rule (Pred &&p, Trans &&t)
{
  return RewriteRule<Tag, std::decay_t<Pred>, std::decay_t<Trans>>{
    std::forward<Pred> (p), std::forward<Trans> (t)
  };
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{std::decay_t} is what makes the rule's type independent of how
the callables were passed. A rule built from a function name, a lambda
stored in a variable, and a lambda stored in a \code{const} variable all
have the same type, because the decay strips the reference and the
top-level \code{const}. The \code{constexpr} on both the factory and the
constructor means that a rule built from captureless lambdas is a
constant expression, which is what allows \code{constexpr auto} at
namespace scope, as in Listing~\ref{lst:rewrite-demo}.

\subsection{Matching is the predicate's job}

The engine does not match anything. There is no pattern language, no
pattern variable, no substitution, and no unification. A rule fires
exactly when its predicate returns \code{true}, and the predicate is an
ordinary C++ function that inspects whatever it likes.

In practice a predicate inspects the node's tag and the tags and shapes of
its children:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A predicate that matches a specific shape.},label={lst:predicate-shape}]
[] (dsl::ASTNode const &n) {
  return !n.is_leaf () && n.tag () == "add" && n.size () == 2
         && !n.child (0).is_leaf () && n.child (0).tag () == "num"
         && !n.child (1).is_leaf () && n.child (1).tag () == "num";
}
\end{lstlisting}

This is more code than a pattern would be, and it buys several things in
return. The predicate is ordinary C++ and can use any library the user
likes. It can consult state captured by reference --- a symbol table, a
type environment, a counter. It can throw, in which case the whole
rewrite aborts with an exception rather than silently failing to match.
And it can be tested in isolation, without a tree, by calling
\code{rule.pred (node)} on a hand-built node.

The cost is that matching is not composable in the way a pattern language
is. Two predicates that both match \code{add} nodes cannot be combined by
the toolkit; the user must combine them by hand, with \code{&&} or with a
helper.

The header's own example, at lines 6761--6766, is the canonical shape:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The header's example rule: remove a double negation.},label={lst:header-rule-example}]
auto r = dsl::rule<"elim-double-neg">(
    [](const ASTNode& n){ return n.tag()=="not" &&
                          n.child(0).tag()=="not"; },
    [](const ASTNode& n){ return n.child(0).child(0); }
);
\end{lstlisting}

Two things about this example deserve a note. The first is that it calls
\code{n.child(0)} without checking \code{n.size()}, and therefore relies
on the tree always having the shape it expects; a tree that reached this
rule with a \code{not} node of arity zero would throw
\code{std::out_of_range}, as \chapref{ch:traversal} explained. The
second is that the predicate does not check that \code{n} is an inner node
at all --- a leaf's \code{tag()} is compared happily --- so a
\code{not}-tagged \emph{leaf} would satisfy the first half of the
conjunction and then throw on \code{n.child(0)}. The example is short
because it is an illustration, not a specification.

\subsection{\code{std::optional<ASTNode>} as the no-match signal}

The return type of \api{try_apply} is \code{std::optional<ASTNode>}. The
optional distinguishes two outcomes that would otherwise be
indistinguishable: the predicate rejected the node, and the predicate
accepted it but the transformer produced a node that happens to be equal
to the input. In the first case the caller learns that the rule does not
apply; in the second the caller learns that the rule applies and what it
produced.

The transformer must return an \api{ASTNode} \emph{by value}. It cannot
return nothing, it cannot return a reference, and it cannot return a
different type. A transformer that wants to express "no change" must
return the node it was given --- which the engine will then treat as a
change, because the rule fired. This is an important subtlety and
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} returns to it.

\section{The \code{Rewrite} feature mixin}

The feature tag \api{dsl::Rewrite} is declared at line 6922. Its mixin
contributes exactly one member:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{5.4cm}C{1.0cm}L{6.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Line} & \textbf{Semantics} \\
\midrule
\code{ASTNode rewrite(const ASTNode& tree) const}
  & 6934
  & Applies \code{Derived::rules} to \code{tree} and returns the result.
    The call is forwarded to \code{Derived::rules.apply (tree)}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The \code{Rewrite} feature mixin. Its single member is guarded
by a nested requirement expression that demands a \texttt{rules} member
on the derived class.}
\label{tab:rewrite-mixin}
\end{table}

The guard is worth reading carefully:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The rewrite member and its constraint.},label={lst:rewrite-mixin-impl}]
ASTNode
rewrite (const ASTNode &tree) const
  requires requires { Derived::rules; }
{
  return Derived::rules.apply (tree);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The constraint is a \emph{nested} requirement expression. The inner
\code{requires \{ Derived::rules; \}} checks that the expression
\code{Derived::rules} is well formed --- that the derived class has a
member called \code{rules} and that it is accessible from the mixin. It
does \emph{not} check the type of that member, and it does not check that
it has an \code{apply} member. The constraint is satisfied by an
\code{int}, and the failure then occurs inside the function body, as a
hard error rather than as a substitution failure:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A mis-typed \code{rules} member fails in the body, not in the constraint.},label={lst:rules-int}]
struct Bad : dsl::DSL<Bad, dsl::Rewrite> {
  static inline int rules = 42;
};
// error: request for member 'apply' in 'Bad::rules',
//        which is of non-class type 'int'
\end{lstlisting}

This is a defensible design --- the constraint's job is to say "this mixin
requires the derived class to have a \code{rules} member", and the
diagnostic for a wrongly typed member is clear enough --- but it is worth
knowing that the constraint will not protect a user from a typo in the
member's type.

One more detail about the mixin's member: it is \code{const}, and it takes
the tree by \code{const ASTNode&}. The tree is therefore not copied on
the way in --- but it \emph{is} copied inside, because
\api{RewriteSet::apply} takes its argument by value. The rewrite of
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} explains why, and what it costs.

The mixin also does not require \code{Derived::rules} to be static. The
header's example uses a static member, and a static member is the natural
choice because the rule set is normally the same for every instance of the
DSL; but a non-static member would satisfy the constraint, and the call
\code{Derived::rules.apply (tree)} would then read the member of the
object on which \code{rewrite} was called.

The mixin requires nothing of the derived class beyond the existence of
\code{rules}. It does not require the member to be static, although the
example in the header uses a static one; it does not require the member to
be a \api{RewriteSet}; and it does not require the derived class to be
complete at the point of the mixin's definition.

\subsection{Writing predicates that do not crash}

Because a predicate is ordinary code, it is ordinary code's problem to
check its preconditions. The failure mode is specific and unforgiving: a
predicate that calls \code{n.child(0)} on a node with no children throws
\code{std::out_of_range}, and the exception propagates out of the rewrite
pass, out of \api{apply}, and out of the user's program. Nothing catches
it, and the diagnostic names a vector rather than a rule.

The defensive style that works is a sequence of short-circuiting guards,
each of which establishes one more fact about the node:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A predicate that establishes its preconditions one at a time.},label={lst:guarded-predicate}]
[] (dsl::ASTNode const &n) {
  if (n.is_leaf ())          // not an inner node
    return false;
  if (n.tag () != "add")     // wrong tag
    return false;
  if (n.size () != 2)        // wrong arity
    return false;
  if (!n.child (0).is_leaf () || n.child (0).tag () != "num")
    return false;
  return n.child (1).is_leaf () && n.child (1).tag () == "num";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The order matters. The cheapest and most selective test comes first, so
that a node of the wrong shape is rejected before any child is touched.
Factoring the repeated shape test into a helper --- as the
\code{tag_is} and \code{is_num} functions of
Listing~\ref{lst:rewrite-demo} do --- keeps the predicate readable and
makes the guards consistent between rules.

\section{Type erasure and run-time rule lists}

The type-level encoding of a rule set has an immediate consequence: a
\api{RewriteSet} has a fixed, compile-time number of rules. There is no
\code{RewriteSet<std::vector<Rule>>} and no way to build a set whose size
is known only at run time, because the set's template arguments are the
rule types.

Type erasure is nevertheless available, by choosing \code{std::function}
as the predicate and transformer types when the rule is created:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A type-erased rule.},label={lst:erased-rule}]
using Pred  = std::function<bool (dsl::ASTNode const &)>;
using Trans = std::function<dsl::ASTNode (dsl::ASTNode const &)>;

auto r = dsl::rule<"erased"> (
    Pred  ([](dsl::ASTNode const &n) { return !n.is_leaf () && n.tag () == "not"; }),
    Trans ([](dsl::ASTNode const &n) { return n.child (0); }));
\end{lstlisting}

This compiles and behaves exactly as the un-erased rule does. What is lost
is the fine-grained type identity: every type-erased rule built with the
same tag has the same type, because the closure types have been replaced
by the two \code{std::function} types. A run-time list of rules is
therefore expressible --- as a \code{std::vector} of such rules, applied
one at a time by the user --- but it is not a \api{RewriteSet}, and it
does not enjoy the fixpoint loop of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} without
additional machinery.

\section{The type-level encoding}

Every rule object has a distinct C++ type, and the type encodes three
things: the tag, the predicate's type, and the transformer's type. Two
rules built with the same tag but different lambdas have different types,
because the closure types differ; two rules built from the same two
lambdas have the same type.

This matters because \api{RewriteSet} of \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} stores
its rules in a \code{std::tuple<Rules...>}, whose element types are
template arguments. The type of a rule set is therefore a compile-time
record of exactly which rules it contains and in what order. Two sets with
the same rules in a different order have different types, and a set with
one extra rule has a different type again.

That encoding buys three things. It makes the rule list inspectable at
compile time: \code{std::tuple_size_v} of the set's \code{rules} member
is the number of rules, and the set's type can be named. It makes the set
a distinct type per configuration, so that a function taking
\code{RewriteSet<R1, R2>} cannot be handed a set with different rules by
accident. And it means the engine can be written with plain \code{std::apply}
over a tuple, with no dynamic dispatch and no type erasure --- which is
exactly what the shipped implementation does.

\section{The flow of a single rule}

\figref{fig:rule-flow} shows what happens when one rule is offered one
node.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dsllarge] (node) {ASTNode \code{n}};

  \node[dslbox, below=9mm of node] (pred)
    {\\code{rule.pred (n)}\\user-supplied predicate};
  \node[dsldecision, below=9mm of pred] (test)
    {\\code{pred (n)} true?};

  \node[dslbox, below=9mm of test, xshift=-24mm] (no)
    {\\code{std::nullopt}\\no match};
  \node[dslbox, below=9mm of test, xshift=24mm] (yes)
    {\\code{rule.trans (n)}\\user-supplied transformer};

  \node[dslbox, below=9mm of yes] (out)
    {\\code{std::optional}\\\code{<ASTNode>}\\holds the replacement};

  \draw[dsledge] (node) -- (pred);
  \draw[dsledge] (pred) -- (test);
  \draw[dsledge] (test) -- node[above] {no} (no);
  \draw[dsledge] (test) -- node[below] {yes} (yes);
  \draw[dsledge] (yes) -- (out);
  \draw[dslfeedback] (out.east) to[out=0, in=0] (no.east);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The control flow of \texttt{try\_apply}. The predicate is called
exactly once; the transformer is called only if the predicate accepted.}
\label{fig:rule-flow}
\end{figure}

Two paths leave \api{try_apply}. On the left, the predicate rejected the
node and the function returns an empty optional. On the right, the
transformer produced a replacement and the function returns an optional
holding it. There is no third path: the transformer is not called when the
predicate rejects, and there is no way for a rule to report an error other
than by throwing.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\paragraph{There is no \code{apply} and no \code{matches}.}
The single method is \code{try_apply}. A user who wants a boolean test
calls the public \code{pred} member; a user who wants to transform
unconditionally calls the public \code{trans} member. Neither is wrapped.

\paragraph{The tag is never read by the engine.}
\code{RewriteRule::name} exists, is public, and is a constant expression.
Nothing in the engine consults it: the engine calls \code{try_apply} and
nothing else. The tag's real function is to distinguish rule \emph{types},
which is what lets a rule set record its contents at compile time.

\paragraph{Predicates and transformers are stored by value.}
Both callables are copied into the rule. A rule that captures a large
object by value carries that object; a rule that captures by reference
carries a reference, and the referenced object must outlive the rule. A
static rule set that captures a local variable by reference is a dangling
reference waiting to happen.

\paragraph{The transformer must return an \api{ASTNode}.}
There is no \code{std::optional} return type on the transformer and no way
to signal "leave this node alone". A transformer that returns the node it
was given will be treated by the engine as having changed the node, which
is what makes the engine's fixpoint test conservative. \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets}
returns to this.

\paragraph{The predicate is called once per node per pass.}
There is no caching, no memoisation, and no short-circuit across rules. A
predicate that is expensive is expensive once per node per pass, and the
number of passes is the number of iterations of the fixpoint loop.

\paragraph{No pattern variables, no unification, no substitution.}
The toolkit's \api{pattern} of \chapref{ch:pattern-regex} is a compile-time
string matcher and is not connected to the rewrite machinery. The
e-graph subsystem of \chapref{ch:egraphs} does have variables and
substitution, in the \code{?name} convention of its S-expression rules.

\paragraph{No way to report a failure other than an exception.}
A predicate or transformer that cannot proceed has two options: return
\code{false}, meaning "this rule does not apply", or throw. There is no
diagnostic channel, no error node, and no recovery.

\paragraph{Node kinds are opaque strings.}
Because the tag of an \api{ASTNode} is a runtime string, a predicate
compares it against a string literal at every node it examines. The
comparison is a \code{std::string} comparison, which for short tags is
cheap but not free, and it happens once per rule per node per pass.

\section{Closing}

A rule in \DSLtk{} is a predicate and a transformer, stored by value, with
a name that exists for the reader rather than for the engine. The engine
knows only one thing about a rule: it can be asked whether it applies to a
node, and if so what it would replace that node with. Everything else ---
the shape of the tree the rule matches, the order in which rules are
tried, the number of times they are tried --- is decided by the user and
by the set that holds them. \chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} takes the rule and
builds the set, the pass, and the fixpoint loop around it.
